For the Lecture 3 study activity, replace y\in\{0,1\} with 0\le y\le1. Before solving, predict whether the returned point will be integral or fractional. Then solve and check every binary component against an integrality tolerance and every row against a feasibility tolerance.

Check your interpretation after trying the activity

An integral feasible LP point also solves the integer feasibility model, with objective zero.

A fractional LP point is not a completed board. It does not prove that no integer completion exists. Search, cuts, stronger constraints or a separate completion procedure may be needed to find one.

Both the LP and IP have optimal objective zero whenever feasible. Thus an objective gap of zero gives no integrality information for this constant-objective model.

On an empty n\times n board, assigning y_{v,r,c}=1/n satisfies all four exactly-one families: every sum has n terms. It is fractional for n>1 and optimal for the zero objective. This point alone does not prove that the polytope has a fractional extreme point; it could be a convex combination of integral boards.

An integral return for one puzzle or objective does not establish an integrality theorem for all Sudoku relaxations. The diagnostic below contrasts a given board and an empty board.

Reference: BGW (1997), Appendix A, §A.3.2, pp. 758, 760–761, for integer relaxation and bound quality. For the Sudoku formulation, Bynum et al., Pyomo, 3rd ed., §5.4.2, pp. 76–79.

# Define class 'Puzzle' to store an unsolved or solved sudoku board
# Stores size of puzzle, grid values, and subgrid coordinates


class Puzzle:
    """One sudoku board: its size, its cells, and its subgrid geometry.

    Arguments:
        board: an n-by-n nested list (or array) of ints, 1..n for a filled
            cell and 0 for a blank one
    """

    def __init__(self, board):

        # n = number of rows = number of columns
        n = len(board[0])
        # side length of one subgrid
        sub = int(math.sqrt(n))

        self.size = n
        self.grid = [list(int(v) for v in row) for row in board]

        # s[(v,r,c)] = 1 if the cell in position (r,c) has value v
        # s[(v,r,c)] = 0 otherwise
        # Note: rows and columns begin at 1 and end at n
        self.params = {
            (v, r, c): int(self.grid[r - 1][c - 1] == v)
            for v in range(1, n + 1)
            for r in range(1, n + 1)
            for c in range(1, n + 1)
        }

        # subgrids[i] = collection of ordered pairs (r,c) such that (r,c) is in
        # the i-th subgrid. Subgrids are sqrt(n) by sqrt(n) and are indexed top
        # to bottom, then left to right.
        self.subgrids = {
            i: [
                (r, c)
                for r in range(1 + sub * ((i - 1) % sub), 1 + sub * ((i - 1) % sub + 1))
                for c in range(
                    1 + sub * ((i - 1) // sub),
                    1 + sub * ((i - 1) // sub + 1),
                )
            ]
            for i in range(1, n + 1)
        }

