At a fixed nominal parameter, write the new information as \mathbf A^T\mathbf A\succeq0 and suppose \mathbf M_0\succ0. Let \mathbf B=\mathbf A\mathbf M_0^{-1/2}. Then\begin{aligned}
\mathbf M_1&=\mathbf M_0+\mathbf A^T\mathbf A
=\mathbf M_0^{1/2}(\mathbf I+\mathbf B^T\mathbf B)\mathbf M_0^{1/2},\\
\mathbf M_1^{-1}
&=\mathbf M_0^{-1/2}(\mathbf I+\mathbf B^T\mathbf B)^{-1}\mathbf M_0^{-1/2}
\preceq\mathbf M_0^{-1}.
\end{aligned}

The eigenvalues of (\mathbf I+\mathbf B^T\mathbf B)^{-1} lie in (0,1], so predicted variance cannot increase in any fixed direction. Equality may remain in directions the new experiment does not inform. Recomputing sensitivities at a different refitted parameter changes the premise: an observed covariance need not match this fixed-parameter prediction.

For one scalar measurement, set \mathbf u=\mathbf q^T/\sigma and use the rank-one formulas\frac{\det(\mathbf M_0+\mathbf u\mathbf u^T)}{\det\mathbf M_0}
=1+\mathbf u^T\mathbf M_0^{-1}\mathbf u,
\qquad
(\mathbf M_0+\mathbf u\mathbf u^T)^{-1}
=\mathbf M_0^{-1}
-\frac{\mathbf M_0^{-1}\mathbf u\mathbf u^T\mathbf M_0^{-1}}
{1+\mathbf u^T\mathbf M_0^{-1}\mathbf u}.

The D-optimal next measurement therefore maximizes its sensitivity in the existing covariance metric, \mathbf q\widehat{\mathbf V}\mathbf q^T/\sigma^2. Merely choosing the largest Euclidean sensitivity norm ignores information already collected. These identities apply to the local additive-information model used here; they are not guarantees about the realized refit.

Given a function f(\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta}) where \mathbf{x} is the input or array of inputs, \boldsymbol{\theta} is the vector of unknown model parameters, and \mathbf{y} is the array of observed output, parameter estimation is performed to determine the values of \boldsymbol{\theta} to minimize the error between f(\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta}) and \mathbf{y}.  Commonly, parameter estimation is set up as a least squares objective problem:\begin{align}
\begin{split}
    \min_{\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}} \quad & \sum_{i}^{} (y_i - f(\mathbf{x}_i,\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}))^2\\
    \textrm{s.t.} \quad & \mathrm{bounds \ on} \ \boldsymbol{\theta}\\
    & \mathrm{other \ physical \ constraints}\\
\end{split}
\end{align}

where i is used to index the datapoints in a dataset and \hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}} is the optimal set of parameter values that minimizes the prediction error.

Here, we will estimate parameters A_1, A_2, E_1, and E_2 using data generated for a batch ‘experiment’ at 250 K with an inlet concentration of 0.5 mol/L of A.

The parameter estimation problem is now defined to solve optimization problem where the objective function is the mean of the least square error between observed and calculated data for multiple experiments.\begin{align}
\begin{split}
    \min_{\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}} \quad & \frac{1}{N}\sum_{j}^{}\sum_{i}^{} (y_{j,i} - f(\mathbf{x}_{j,i},\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}))^2\\
    \textrm{s.t.} \quad & \mathrm{bounds \ on} \ \boldsymbol{\theta}\\
    & \mathrm{other \ physical \ constraints}\\
\end{split}
\end{align}

where i is used to index the datapoints in a dataset, j is an index on the dataset such that j \in [1,N] and N is the number of experiments conducted.

Assume f is C^3 near a and h>0. Forward, backward and central approximations are\begin{aligned}
D_+(h)&=\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}h,\\
D_-(h)&=\frac{f(a)-f(a-h)}h,\\
D_0(h)&=\frac{f(a+h)-f(a-h)}{2h}.
\end{aligned}

Taylor expansion gives D_+(h)-f'(a)=\tfrac12 f''(a)h+O(h^2) and D_-(h)-f'(a)=-\tfrac12 f''(a)h+O(h^2). For central differences, write both expansions before subtracting:\begin{aligned}
f(a+h)&=f(a)+f'(a)h+\tfrac12f''(a)h^2+O(h^3),\\
f(a-h)&=f(a)-f'(a)h+\tfrac12f''(a)h^2+O(h^3).
\end{aligned}

The constant and second-order terms cancel. Thus f(a+h)-f(a-h)=2f'(a)h+O(h^3) and D_0(h)-f'(a)=O(h^2). With the C^3 expansion to the cubic term, the leading central error is f^{(3)}(a)h^2/6+o(h^2). See Nocedal & Wright (2006), §8.1, pp.195–197.

