For symmetric \mathbf{K}=\left[\begin{smallmatrix}\mathbf{W}&\mathbf{J}_h^T\\\mathbf{J}_h&0\end{smallmatrix}\right], full-row-rank \mathbf{J}_h and positive reduced Hessian give inertia (n,m,0). The theorem refers to the original equality KKT system; a regularized system’s signature has a different interpretation. If needed, solve\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{W}+\delta_W \mathbf{I}&\mathbf{J}_h^T\\\mathbf{J}_h&-\delta_A \mathbf{I}\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf d_x\\\mathbf d_v\end{bmatrix}
=-\begin{bmatrix}\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}L\\h\end{bmatrix},\qquad\delta_W,\delta_A\ge0.

The code uses A = \mathbf{J}_h.T and unchanged finite-difference helpers. Its loop inspects the unmodified signature, chooses a positive dual regularization when an eigenvalue is classified near zero, and grows the primal correction until the numerical signature is (n,m,0) or a limit is reached. It retains the previous successful scale as a starting estimate. Near-zero classification is a numerical diagnosis; a zero can also arise from a singular reduced Hessian rather than a dependent Jacobian. These corrections make a step computable; they do not repair a model or certify the original SOSC. Biegler (5.12), Theorem 5.4 and Algorithm 5.2, pp. 97–99.

