Recall, the step for a primal-dual interior point method is defined by the following system of linear equations (Eq. 6.56 in Biegler, 2010):\begin{bmatrix}
\mathbf{W}^k & \nabla \mathbf{c}(\mathbf{x}^k) & -\mathbf{I} \\
\nabla \mathbf{c}(\mathbf{x}^k)^T & \mathbf{0} & \mathbf{0} \\
\mathbf{U}^k & \mathbf{0} & \mathbf{X}^k
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
\mathbf{d}_x^k \\
\mathbf{d}_v^k \\
\mathbf{d}_u^k
\end{bmatrix}
=
-\begin{bmatrix}
\nabla f(\mathbf{x}^k) + \nabla \mathbf{c}(\mathbf{x}^k) \mathbf{v}^k - \mathbf{u}^k \\
\mathbf{c}(\mathbf{x}^k) \\
\mathbf{X}^k \mathbf{u}^k - \mu_l \mathbf{e}
\end{bmatrix}.

Alternatively, the step for the dual variables for the bounds can be recommed for this system (Eq. 6.57 in Biegler, 2010):\begin{bmatrix}
\mathbf{W}^k + \boldsymbol{\Sigma}^k & \nabla \mathbf{c}(\mathbf{x}^k) \\
\nabla \mathbf{c}(\mathbf{x}^k)^T & \mathbf{0}
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
\mathbf{d}_x^k \\
\mathbf{d}_v^k
\end{bmatrix}
=
-\begin{bmatrix}
\nabla \varphi_\mu(\mathbf{x}^k) + \nabla \mathbf{c}(\mathbf{x}^k) \mathbf{v}^k \\
\mathbf{c}(\mathbf{x}^k)
\end{bmatrix}.

And then computed using the solution to the linear system (Eq. 6.58 in Biegler, 2010):\mathbf{d}_u^k = \mu_l (\mathbf{X}^k)^{-1} \mathbf{e} - \mathbf{u}^k - \boldsymbol{\Sigma}^k \mathbf{d}_x^k.

Finally, inertia correction and regularization can be applied to ensure reliable calculation of the Newton step (Eq. 6.59 in Biegler, 2010):\begin{bmatrix}
\mathbf{W}^k + \boldsymbol{\Sigma}^k + \delta_W \mathbf{I} & \nabla \mathbf{c}(\mathbf{x}^k) \\
\nabla \mathbf{c}(\mathbf{x}^k)^T & -\delta_A \mathbf{I}
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
\mathbf{d}_x^k \\
\mathbf{d}_v^k
\end{bmatrix}
=
-\begin{bmatrix}
\nabla \varphi_\mu(\mathbf{x}^k) + \nabla \mathbf{c}(\mathbf{x}^k) \mathbf{v}^k \\
\mathbf{c}(\mathbf{x}^k)
\end{bmatrix}.

Consider the following nonlinear program:\begin{align*}
	\min_{\mathbf{x}} \quad & f(\mathbf{x}) \\
	\mathrm{s.t.} \quad & \mathbf{c}(\mathbf{x}) = \mathbf{0} \\
	& x_i \geq 0, \quad i \in \mathcal{I}
\end{align*}

where \mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^{n}, f(\mathbf{x}): \mathbb{R}^{n} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}, \mathbf{c}(\mathbf{x}): \mathbb{R}^{n} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^{m} and |\mathcal{I} | = r (i.e., there are r variables with a lower bound and r \leq n). This is an extension of (6.48) in Biegler (2010).

This has the corresponding log-barrier approximation:\begin{align*}
	\min_{\mathbf{x}} \quad & \phi_{\mu_l}(\mathbf{x}) := f(\mathbf{x}) - \mu_l \sum_{i \in \mathcal{I}} \log(x_i) \\
	\mathrm{s.t.} \quad & \mathbf{c}(\mathbf{x}) = \mathbf{0}
\end{align*}

which is an extension of (6.49) in Biegler (2010).

Let the n \times r matrix \mathbf{G} encode which variables are bounded. If variable i corresponds to the jth bound, then G_{i,j} = 1 and otherwise G_{i,j} = 0.

\mathbf{G} is assembled as follows:

Initialize \mathbf{G} as the zero matrix

Loop over k=1 to k=|\mathcal{I}|

Extract the index i corresponding to the element \mathcal{I}_k

Set G_{i,k} = 1

Notice that \mathbf{G} is the gradient of \mathbf{x} \geq \mathbf{0}. \mathbf{G}^T \mathbf{G} = \mathbf{I} but the converse does not hold unless r = n.

Start with:\begin{align*}
	\min_{\mathbf{x}} \quad & x_1^2 + x_2 \\
	\mathrm{s.t.} \quad & x_1 + x_2 = 1 \\
	& x_1 + 1 \geq 0
\end{align*}

Add slack variable x_3 and convert the inequality constraint to an equality constraint and bound:\begin{align*}
	\min_{\mathbf{x}} \quad & x_1^2 + x_2 \\
	\mathrm{s.t.} \quad & x_1 + x_2 = 1 \\
	& x_1 + 1 - x_3 = 0 \\
	& x_3 \geq 0
\end{align*}

Now assemble \mathbf{G}:\mathbf{G} = \begin{bmatrix}
		0 \\
		0 \\
		1
	\end{bmatrix}

Next we extend (6.51) in Biegler (2010):\begin{gather*}
	\underbrace{\nabla f(\mathbf{x}^k)}_{n \times 1} ~+~ \underbrace{\nabla \mathbf{c}(\mathbf{x}^k)}_{n \times m} \underbrace{\mathbf{v}^k}_{m \times 1} ~-~ \underbrace{\mathbf{G}}_{n \times r} \underbrace{\mathbf{u}^k}_{r \times 1} = \underbrace{\mathbf{0}}_{n \times 1} \\
	\underbrace{\hat{\mathbf{X}}^k}_{r \times r} \underbrace{\mathbf{u}^k}_{r \times 1} = \mu \underbrace{\mathbf{e}}_{r \times 1} \\
	\underbrace{\mathbf{c}(\mathbf{x}^k)}_{m \times 1} = \underbrace{\mathbf{0}}_{m \times 1}
\end{gather*}

We now extend (6.56) in Biegler (2010):\begin{bmatrix}
		\underbrace{\mathbf{W}^k}_{n \times n} & \underbrace{\nabla \mathbf{c}(\mathbf{x}^k)}_{n \times m} & -\underbrace{\mathbf{G}}_{n \times r} \\
		\underbrace{\nabla_{\mathbf{x}} \mathbf{c}(\mathbf{x}^k)^T}_{m \times n} & \underbrace{\mathbf{0}}_{m \times m} & \underbrace{\mathbf{0}}_{m \times r} \\
		\underbrace{\mathbf{U}^k}_{r \times r} \underbrace{\mathbf{G}^T}_{r \times n} & \underbrace{\mathbf{0}}_{r \times m} & \underbrace{\hat{\mathbf{X}}^k}_{r \times r}
	\end{bmatrix} 
	\begin{bmatrix}
		\underbrace{\mathbf{d}_x^k}_{n \times 1} \\
		\underbrace{\mathbf{d}_v^k}_{m \times 1} \\
		\underbrace{\mathbf{d}_u^k}_{r \times 1}
	\end{bmatrix} = -
	\begin{bmatrix}
	\underbrace{\nabla f(\mathbf{x}^k)}_{n \times 1} ~+~ \underbrace{\nabla \mathbf{c}(\mathbf{x}^k)}_{n \times m} \underbrace{\mathbf{v}^k}_{m \times 1} ~-~ \underbrace{\mathbf{G}}_{n \times r} \underbrace{\mathbf{u}^k}_{r \times 1} \\
	\underbrace{\mathbf{c}(\mathbf{x}^k)}_{m \times 1} \\
	\underbrace{\hat{\mathbf{X}}^k}_{r \times r} \underbrace{\mathbf{u}^k}_{r \times 1} ~-~ \mu_l \underbrace{\mathbf{e}}_{r \times 1}
	\end{bmatrix}

where \mathbf{U}^k = \mathrm{diag}\{\mathbf{u}^k\} and \mathbf{W}^k = \nabla_{\mathbf{xx}} L(\mathbf{x}^k,\mathbf{v}^k). Notice that \mathbf{W}^k does NOT include a contribution from the barrier term:\underbrace{\mathbf{W}^k}_{n \times n} = \underbrace{\nabla^2 f(\mathbf{x}^k)}_{n \times n} + \sum_{j=1}^{m} \left( \underbrace{\nabla^2 c_{j}(\mathbf{x}^k)}_{n \times n} \underbrace{v^k_{j}}_{\mathrm{scalar}} \right)

We can verify that \mathbf{W}^k does not include \hat{\mathbf{X}} by showing that the KKT system above is a Newton step to solve the nonlinear system for the primal dual conditions.

The Newton step can be simplified, similar to (6.57) and (6.58) in Biegler (2010):\begin{bmatrix}
		\underbrace{\mathbf{W}^k}_{n \times n} + \underbrace{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}^k}_{n \times n} & \underbrace{\nabla \mathbf{c}(\mathbf{x}^k)}_{n \times m} \\
		\underbrace{\nabla_{\mathbf{x}} \mathbf{c}(\mathbf{x}^k)^T}_{m \times n} & \mathbf{0}
	\end{bmatrix} 
	\begin{bmatrix}
		\underbrace{\mathbf{d}_x^k}_{n \times 1} \\
		\underbrace{\mathbf{d}_v^k}_{m \times 1}
	\end{bmatrix} = -
	\begin{bmatrix}
	\underbrace{\nabla \phi_{\mu_l}}_{n \times 1} ~+~ \underbrace{\nabla \mathbf{c}(\mathbf{x}^k)}_{n \times m} \underbrace{\mathbf{v}^k}_{m \times 1} \\
	\underbrace{\mathbf{c}(\mathbf{x}^k)}_{m \times 1}
	\end{bmatrix}

and\underbrace{\mathbf{d}_u^k}_{r \times 1} = \mu_l \underbrace{(\hat{\mathbf{X}}^k)^{-1}}_{r \times r} \underbrace{\mathbf{e}}_{r \times 1} ~-~ \underbrace{\mathbf{u}^k}_{r \times 1} ~-~ \underbrace{\mathbf{G}^T}_{r \times n} \underbrace{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}^k}_{n \times n} \underbrace{\mathbf{d}_x^k}_{n \times 1}

where\underbrace{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}^k}_{n \times n} = \underbrace{\mathbf{G}}_{n \times r} \underbrace{(\hat{\mathbf{X}}^k)^{-1}}_{r \times r} \underbrace{\mathbf{U}^k}_{r \times r} \underbrace{\mathbf{G}^T}_{r \times n}

and\underbrace{\nabla \phi_{\mu_l}}_{n \times 1} = \underbrace{\nabla f(\mathbf{x}^k)}_{n \times 1} - \mu_l \underbrace{\mathbf{G}}_{n \times r} \underbrace{(\hat{\mathbf{X}}^k)^{-1}}_{r \times r} \underbrace{\mathbf{e}}_{r \times 1}

Notice that the equation for \underbrace{\mathbf{d}_u^k}_{r \times 1} simplifies by substituting \mathbf{G}^T \mathbf{G} = \mathbf{I}:\underbrace{\mathbf{d}_u^k}_{r \times 1} = \mu_l \underbrace{(\hat{\mathbf{X}}^k)^{-1}}_{r \times r} \underbrace{\mathbf{e}}_{r \times 1} ~-~ \underbrace{\mathbf{u}^k}_{r \times 1} ~-~ \underbrace{(\hat{\mathbf{X}}^k)^{-1}}_{r \times r} \underbrace{\mathbf{U}^k}_{r \times r} \underbrace{\mathbf{G}^T}_{r \times n} \underbrace{\mathbf{d}_x^k}_{n \times 1}

